## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of partition DP with sliding window optimization and modular arithmetic, plus dual implementation styles.

## Key Concepts

- Contiguous partitions
- Sliding window to enforce per-session bound
- Prefix-sum style accumulation for O(n) time

## Code Example

```javascript
function bottomUpCount(a, B) {
  const n = a.length, MOD = 1000000007;
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  let left = 0, window = 0, acc = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    window += a[i-1];
    while (window > B) {
      window -= a[left];
      acc = (acc - dp[left] + MOD) % MOD;
      left++;
    }
    acc = (acc + dp[i-1]) % MOD;
    dp[i] = acc;
  }
  return dp[n];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify for 64‑bit sums to avoid overflow in JS/TS?
- How does this pattern relate to monotone queue optimizations in similar counting problems?

## Why This Is Asked
This question blends BFS reachability, Dijkstra shortest paths, and counting shortest paths via a DAG and topological order.

## Key Concepts
- BFS/DFS reachability
- Dijkstra's algorithm
- Shortest-path DAG
- Topological DP and modular counting

## Code Example
```javascript
function countShortestPaths(n, adj, s) {
  const dist = Array(n).fill(Infinity); dist[s] = 0;
  // priority queue based Dijkstra (pseudo-notation)
  const pq = new MinPQ((a,b)=>a[0]-b[0]);
  pq.enqueue([0,s]);
  while(!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [d,u] = pq.dequeue(); if (d!==dist[u]) continue;
    for (const {to:v, w} of adj[u]){
      if (dist[v] > d + w){ dist[v] = d + w; pq.enqueue([dist[v], v]); }
    }
  }
  const dag = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>[]);
  for (let u=0; u<n; u++) for (const {to:v, w} of adj[u]) if (dist[u] + w === dist[v]) dag[u].push(v);
  const order = Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=>i).sort((a,b)=>dist[a]-dist[b]);
  const MOD = 1000000007; const ways = Array(n).fill(0); ways[s] = 1;
  for (const u of order) for (const v of dag[u]) ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % MOD;
  return {dist, ways};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would zero-weight edges affect the approach? 
- How to extend to count modulo a large prime or handle multiple sources?

## Why This Is Asked
Beginner DP concepts with a new angle: counting unordered combinations (coin change ways) and contrasting memoization with tabulation.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming (top-down memoization)
- Tabulation (bottom-up DP)
- Unordered combinations vs permutations
- Space optimization (1D DP)

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWaysTopDown(coins, target) {
  const n = coins.length;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, rem) {
    if (rem === 0) return 1;
    if (rem < 0 || i === n) return 0;
    const key = i + '|' + rem;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i + 1, rem) + dfs(i, rem - coins[i]);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0, target);
}

function countWaysBottomUp(coins, target) {
  const dp = new Array(target + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const c of coins) {
    for (let r = c; r <= target; r++) {
      dp[r] += dp[r - c];
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you count permutations (order matters) instead of combinations?
- How would you handle very large targets or mod results to avoid overflow in languages with fixed integer sizes?
